# Keep Airflow's plugin scanner out of the datagates package.
#
# Airflow imports every .py file under plugins/ using a module name taken from
# the file's *basename*, and registers it in sys.modules under that name. Two
# things went wrong here before this file existed:
#
#   * datagates/core/http.py was imported as `http`, shadowing the standard
#     library for the entire process. Everything that then did
#     `from http import HTTPStatus` -- urllib3, requests, Airflow itself --
#     failed, DAG parsing produced zero files, and `airflow dags list-import-
#     errors` reported nothing at all, because the failure happened before any
#     DAG file was read. (The module is now called httpclient.py, but the trap
#     applies to any name the standard library also uses: json, logging,
#     types, select, socket, email, csv...)
#   * datagates/__main__.py was imported as `__main__`, so its
#     `if __name__ == "__main__"` guard was true and the CLI parsed Airflow's
#     own argv: `airflow users create` died with "invalid choice: 'users'".
#
# Nothing in datagates is an Airflow plugin -- it declares no AirflowPlugin --
# and the package is imported normally through PYTHONPATH, which
# docker-compose sets to /opt/airflow/plugins. So there is nothing here for
# the scanner to find and every reason to keep it out.
datagates/
